course: books
lesson: 68
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter XI, part 4: «Like Summer Tempests Came His Tears» (the secret passage, the birthday banquet and «learn 'em»)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Badger's great secret: there is an underground passage from the river-bank right up into the middle of Toad Hall. Toad says that's nonsense, he knows every inch of his house. But Toad's father found the old passage, cleaned it out and showed it to Badger — with orders not to tell his son, who «simply cannot hold his tongue», unless he was ever in a real fix. Toad sulks for a moment, then admits he is «a bit of a talker».
explain: Badger has also sent Otter, disguised as a chimney-sweep, to the back door. Tomorrow night is the Chief Weasel's birthday banquet: all the weasels will be feasting in the dining-hall with no weapons, trusting their sentries outside. And the passage comes up under the butler's pantry, right next to the dining-hall! «Aha! that squeaky board!» cries Toad.
explain: Off to bed. Toad dreams of roads running away from him, canals chasing him, a barge sailing into his dinner-party with his washing, and the secret passage twisting and sitting up on its end — but at last he is home and safe, and everybody tells him he is clever.
explain: Next morning Toad comes down late. The Mole has slipped off somewhere by himself without telling anyone; the Rat is busily making four little heaps of weapons — «Here's-a-sword-for-the-Rat, here's-a-sword-for-the-Mole...» — while Badger reads the paper and says that, once past the guns, four sticks will clear the hall in five minutes. Toad swings a stick: «I'll learn 'em to steal my house!» The Rat says it should be «teach 'em»; Badger says they don't want to teach them, they want to learn them — and the poor Rat ends up muttering «Learn 'em, teach 'em, teach 'em, learn 'em!» in a corner.
fact: Secrets attracted Toad «because he never could keep one», and he enjoyed the thrill of telling one after promising not to.
fact: «There—is—an—underground—passage,» said the Badger, «that leads from the river-bank, quite near here, right up into the middle of Toad Hall.»
fact: Toad: «You've been listening to some of the yarns they spin in the public-houses about here. I know every inch of Toad Hall» (yarns = made-up stories; a public-house = a pub).
fact: Toad's father «discovered that passage—he didn't make it, of course; that was done hundreds of years before» — and «repaired it and cleaned it out».
fact: The father's words: «He's a good boy, but very light and volatile in character, and simply cannot hold his tongue. If he's ever in a real fix... you may tell him about the secret passage; but not before.» (volatile = changing his mind and mood quickly)
fact: Toad brightens: «perhaps I am a bit of a talker... I have the gift of conversation. I've been told I ought to have a salon, whatever that may be.» (a salon = a gathering of clever people for conversation)
fact: Otter dressed up as a sweep (a chimney-sweep) with brushes over his shoulder and asked at the back door for a job — and so found out the news.
fact: The banquet is tomorrow night for the Chief Weasel's birthday; the weasels will have «No guns, no swords, no sticks, no arms of any sort whatever!» and will trust their sentinels.
fact: The tunnel leads up «under the butler's pantry, next to the dining-hall»; Toad: «Aha! that squeaky board in the butler's pantry! Now I understand it!»
fact: The plan, told in turns — Badger: «We shall creep out quietly into the butler's pantry—» Rat: «—with our pistols and swords and sticks—» Mole: «—and rush in upon them,» Toad: «—and whack 'em, and whack 'em, and whack 'em!» — and Toad runs round and round the room, jumping over the chairs.
fact: After «plain straw... spread on the stone floor of a draughty cell», the sheets and blankets felt «very friendly and comforting things».
fact: The Rat runs round with weapons for «four little heaps»: «Here's-a-sword-for-the-Rat, here's-a-sword-for-the-Mole, here's-a-sword-for-the-Toad, here's-a-sword-for-the-Badger!», then the same with pistols.
fact: Badger: «just let us once get past the stoats, with those detestable guns of theirs, and I assure you we shan't want any swords or pistols. We four, with our sticks... shall clear the floor of all the lot of them in five minutes.»
fact: «I'll learn 'em to steal my house!» In standard English you teach someone and you learn something yourself; «learn 'em» is country dialect meaning «teach them a lesson». Badger: «It's the same what I use myself».
fact: Talk in this lesson: «spill the tea» = tell the gossip; «ken» (Scotland) = know; «snitch» = tell on someone; «big yikes» = that's bad; «mardy» (Midlands) = sulky.
exercise: Why did secrets attract Toad so much? | Because he never could keep one | Because he loved puzzles | Because he wanted to be a detective | Because his father had told him lots of secrets when he was young
exercise: What was Badger's great secret? | A secret passage into Toad Hall | A hidden boat | A gun in the cellar | A spy among the stoats who reports to Badger every night by the river
exercise: What did Toad say at first about the passage? | Nonsense — he knew every inch of Toad Hall | He had used it as a boy | He had heard of it in prison | He was frightened of tunnels and would never go into one
exercise: «Yarns they spin in the public-houses.» What are «yarns» here? | Made-up stories | Balls of wool | Songs | Long ropes that sailors and bargemen use on boats
exercise: Who made the passage? | People hundreds of years ago | Toad's father, as a young man | Badger, long before Toad was born | The weasels, to get into the Hall secretly
exercise: What did Toad's father do with the passage? | Repaired it, cleaned it out, and showed it to Badger | Blocked it up with stones so that nobody could ever get in | Sold it | Used it to store wine
exercise: «Simply cannot hold his tongue.» What does this mean? | He cannot keep a secret | He bites his tongue | He cannot speak | He sticks his tongue out at people he does not like
exercise: When was Badger allowed to tell Toad about the passage? | Only if Toad was ever in a real fix | On Toad's birthday, as a special present | When Toad was grown up and could be trusted | Never, not even if Toad Hall were on fire
exercise: How did Toad take the news that his father hadn't trusted him? | Sulky at first, then he brightened up | He cried all night | He stormed out | He laughed and said his father had been completely right about everything
exercise: «I ought to have a salon, whatever that may be.» What does «whatever that may be» show? | Toad doesn't even know what the word means | Toad knows exactly what it means and is showing off | A salon is a boat | Toad is joking about hairdressers
exercise: How did Badger find out about the banquet? | Otter disguised himself as a sweep and asked at the back door | Mole crept up and listened under the dining-hall window all night | A weasel told him | He read about it in a newspaper
exercise: Whose birthday was it? | The Chief Weasel's | Toad's, since it's his Hall | The Sergeant of the stoats' | Badger's, the oldest friend
exercise: Why was the banquet a chance for the friends? | The weasels would be in the dining-hall with no weapons at all | The weasels would all be asleep upstairs, tired out from dancing | The stoats would go home | The police would come to the party as guests
exercise: Where does the passage come up? | Under the butler's pantry | In the boat-house | In Toad's bedroom | In the stables, behind the red motor-car
exercise: «Aha! that squeaky board!» What did Toad suddenly understand? | Why a board in the butler's pantry always squeaked | Why the big front stairs always squeak when somebody comes up at night | Why the doors stick | Why the weasels sing
exercise: Who said «—and whack 'em, and whack 'em, and whack 'em!»? | Toad | Badger | The Mole | The Rat, polishing his pistol
exercise: What did Toad do after the plan was settled? | Ran round the room jumping over the chairs | Wrote it down | Went quietly to bed | Started telling everybody in the village about the passage
exercise: Which of these did Toad NOT dream about? | Driving a motor-car | Roads running away from him | A barge sailing into his banqueting-hall | The secret passage sitting up on its end
exercise: Where was Mole the next morning? | He had slipped off without telling anyone | Still fast asleep upstairs in the Rat's spare bedroom | Cooking breakfast for Toad, who was late | Rowing
exercise: What was Badger doing that morning? | Reading the paper in an arm-chair | Sharpening swords | Digging | Drawing a big map of the passage on the kitchen table
exercise: How many little heaps of weapons did the Rat make? | Four | Only two | Three | One huge heap, enough for an army of badgers
exercise: What did Badger say they would really need inside the dining-hall? | Just their four sticks | Swords from Toad's armoury | Pistols loaded and ready | Every weapon the Rat could carry, and more
exercise: «I'll learn 'em to steal my house!» What did the Rat say it should be? | «Teach 'em» | «Learn them» | «Steal 'em» | «Show 'em»
exercise: What does «learn 'em» mean the way Toad and Badger use it? | Teach them a lesson | Study them carefully | Read all about them in a big book from the library | Sing them a cheerful song
exercise: What was the Rat muttering in the corner? | «Learn 'em, teach 'em, teach 'em, learn 'em!» | «Here's-a-sword-for-the-Rat, here's-a-pistol-for-the-Mole» | «Poop-poop» | «Toad, Toad, Toad»
test: Max, big yikes (that's bad): «Toad's dad didn't trust him with a secret?! That's harsh.» Why didn't he? | Toad «cannot hold his tongue» | His dad didn't like him and wanted Badger to have the house | It was only for badgers | Toad was too young to walk
test: Why is it funny that Toad «knows every inch» of Toad Hall? | A whole tunnel under it was hidden from him | He really does know every inch, so Badger must be wrong | He has never been inside | He built it himself
test: What would happen if Toad told someone about the passage before tonight? | The weasels might hear and the plan would fail | Nothing at all, because nobody would believe him anyway | The police would help | The passage would close
test: Sophie, spill the tea (tell the gossip): «so Badger snuck into Toad Hall himself to find out about the party?» Put it right. | No: Otter went to the back door disguised as a sweep | Yes, in a washerwoman's dress | No: Toad read it in the paper | Yes, through the passage
test: Why are the weasels «suspecting nothing» a big help? | They will be feasting with no weapons | They will all be asleep in bed after a long day of guarding | They will be out hunting | They will invite Toad
test: Is it TRUE or FALSE? «Badger thinks they need every sword and pistol the Rat has.» | False: four sticks will do, he says | True: he wants even more, and sends Rat out to buy guns | True: he carries six pistols | False: he wants guns only
test: Arlo, «I dinnae ken» (I don't know): «what's wrong with 'learn 'em' anyway?» Best answer? | Standard English says «teach 'em»; «learn 'em» is dialect | Nothing at all, it is the best English, the kind they teach at Oxford | «Learn» is a French word | It should be «learnt 'em»
test: Why does the Rat get «rather muddled» about learn and teach? | Badger insists so firmly that Rat starts to doubt | Rat is tired | Rat never went to school, so he does not really know | Rat is hard of hearing
test: Looking back: in chapter IV, what did Badger say about the old underground city under the Wild Wood? | People came and built and went; the badgers stayed | Badgers built it all, every arch and pillar | It was a palace for toads | It was flooded
test: Looking back: in chapter III, how did Rat find Badger's door in the snow? | A door-scraper, then a door-mat, then the door | A rabbit showed them the way and then ran down a friendly burrow | Badger called out | They followed the smoke
test: Looking back: in chapter IV, how did Badger send Rat and Mole home? | Through a long tunnel to the edge of the wood | In a boat | On a sledge pulled by the two young hedgehogs | Over the hill by moonlight
test: Looking back: in chapter VIII, Toad escaped prison in disguise. How is tonight's plan the same and different? | Same: a secret way past guards; different: friends together | Exactly the same plan, down to the bonnet and shawl | They will also dress up as washerwomen | Toad will go alone
test: Looking back: in chapter VI, Rat was tricked by Toad «playing at being» ill. How does Badger now handle Toad? | He keeps Toad firmly in order | He trusts him completely and lets him go wherever he likes | He lets him plan everything | He sends him to bed for a week
test: Mia's mardy (sulky) little bro: «Toad's useless, why even bring him?» What is the best answer from the story? | It is his house — and Badger knows how to keep him in order | He knows the passage best because he played in it as a boy | He is the best fighter | He has a gun
test: What does Toad's dream about the passage that «twisted and turned round and shook itself» show? | He is excited and nervous about tonight | He is ill | He has been in the passage before and remembers it | He hates tunnels
test: ★★★ The Rat gives each of the four animals one sword and one pistol. How many weapons in the heaps after swords and pistols? | 8 | 4 | 6 | 16
test: ★★★ «Here's-a-sword-for-the-Rat, here's-a-sword-for-the-Mole, here's-a-sword-for-the-Toad, here's-a-sword-for-the-Badger.» In what order does the Rat name them? | Rat, Mole, Toad, Badger | Badger, Rat, Mole, Toad | Toad, Mole, Rat, Badger | Mole, Rat, Badger, Toad
test: ★★★ «But we don't want to teach 'em. We want to learn 'em.» Why is Badger's answer a joke? | He pretends the two words mean different things | Badger can't read, so he never learnt the right words | Badger wants to go to school | Badger is speaking French
exam: Identify the reason assigned by Toad's father for withholding knowledge of the passage from his son. | That his son could not hold his tongue | That his son was too young to be trusted with property | That the passage was unsafe | That the passage belonged to the Badger
exam: Ascertain the means whereby intelligence of the forthcoming banquet was obtained. | The Otter, disguised as a sweep, enquired at the back door | The Mole overheard the sentries conversing at the gate | A penitent weasel confessed | The Rat observed from the river
exam: It is given that the passage terminates beneath a certain chamber. Select the response which correctly identifies that chamber. | The butler's pantry adjoining the dining-hall | The boat-house | The smoking-room where Badger lectured Toad | Toad's bedchamber with the Tudor window
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the dispute between the Rat and the Badger as to «learn 'em»? | The Rat held «teach 'em» correct; the Badger defended «learn 'em» | The Badger conceded that the Rat was correct | Toad adjudicated the matter in his capacity as owner of the Hall | Both agreed upon «show 'em»
